{
  "abstract": "We classify low-genus curves in two complete four-parameter systems on the exotic-quintuple surface of Dujella, Kazalicki and Petričević. The rational hyperplane system $au+bw=cr+d/r+e$ yields exactly their three known positive-rank elliptic families, up to the stated symmetries, among normalized components of genus at most one admitting nondegenerate rational points. Proper sections by $a u^2+2buw+c w^2=A r^2+B+C/r^2$ yield only transforms of the third family; a nonzero mixed coefficient $b$ yields none. The proofs begin with complete plane classifications on two quadric quotients and a coordinate Laurent classification, using multiquadratic branch counting and exact discriminant/resultant stratifications. For the remaining rational hyperplane bases, a repeated-root parametrization and three rank-zero elliptic curves control the branch intersections. A uniform count, including all pole and corner strata, leaves at least eight branch points and forces genus at least three. For quadratic sections, the arbitrary-direction case reduces to an elliptic base whose intersection quartic cannot be a square. Diagonalization over a real quadratic field and conjugation then force any surviving geometric base to split; descent of rational components excludes it. Source-only symbolic certificates and independent arithmetic audits reproduce the exceptional arrangements, rank calculations and diagonalization identities. The contribution is completeness in these specified systems, not discovery of new elliptic families. Arbitrary curves and rational Diophantine septuples remain unresolved.",
